Pension of about ₹30.4 lakh was paid to 127 persons after their death, in one block alone, and more may follow from the review cases. Beyond the money, the finding points to a control failure. The pension sanctioning authority was not receiving, or not acting on, information from the registrar of births and deaths. That systemic weakness is often the more important part of the audit observation.

36.4 A process map
A process map draws traces together as one diagram. Each activity is a box, and each arrow shows how often one activity was directly followed by another. Thick, dark arrows are the main road; thin ones are the side roads. rankdir = "TB" draws the map from top to bottom. Maps are drawn only 300 pixels high by default, which is too small for a process of ten steps, so we write a two-line function to set the height of a map (an htmlwidget, as interactive charts in R are called).

set_height &lt;- function(widget, pixels) {
  widget$height &lt;- pixels
  widget
}

Drawn with every variant at once, a map soon looks like a plate of spaghetti, since every rare deviation adds arrows going back and forth. It is clearer to draw the main road first. filter_trace_frequency(percentage = 0.9) keeps the most frequent traces that together cover 90% of the cases.

purchases |&gt;
  filter_trace_frequency(percentage = 0.9) |&gt;
  process_map(type = frequency("absolute-case"), rankdir = "TB") |&gt;
  set_height(700)


Process map of the most frequent traces, with case counts


This is the prescribed process, as most files follow it: the two routes, with and without a tender, joining at the purchase order, and the loop of bills returned and resubmitted. The map is interactive in the HTML version of this book: it can be zoomed and moved.
Now the opposite: only the files outside the main road, with reverse = TRUE. This is the map an auditor is most interested in.

purchases |&gt;
  filter_trace_frequency(percentage = 0.9, reverse = TRUE) |&gt;
  process_map(type = frequency("absolute-case"), rankdir = "TB") |&gt;
  set_height(700)


Process map of the less frequent traces


Here the deviations stand out: arrows from Indent raised straight to Tender published or to Purchase order issued, skipping the sanction; arrows from Purchase order issued back to Sanction accorded; and arrows from Payment made to Goods received, payment before receipt. Each arrow is labelled with the number of files taking it.
The same map can show time instead of counts. With performance(), each arrow shows the median time between the two activities.

purchases |&gt;
  filter_trace_frequency(percentage = 0.9) |&gt;
  process_map(type = performance(median, "days"), rankdir = "TB") |&gt;
  set_height(700)


Process map with the median days taken between activities


The longest waits are between issuing the purchase order and receiving the goods, and between publishing the tender and evaluating the bids. From indent to payment, a file takes a median of 60 days, and the slowest took 159.






Note



Other filters help to focus a map too: filter_case() for particular files, filter_activity() to keep only some activities, and filter_time_period() for part of the year. A map of each part is usually clearer than one map of everything.

48.4 Cleaning text

48.4.1 Masking personal information
Before anything else, remember that grievances contain personal information, like phone numbers. Before analysing, sharing or quoting such text, mask it. A mobile number in India has ten digits and starts with 6, 7, 8 or 9. The regular expression \\b[6-9][0-9]{9}\\b matches exactly such a number. We learnt regular expressions in Chapter 19.

grievances &lt;- grievances |&gt;
  mutate(text = str_replace_all(text, "\\b[6-9][0-9]{9}\\b", "[PHONE]"))

grievances |&gt;
  filter(str_detect(text, "PHONE")) |&gt;
  slice_head(n = 3) |&gt;
  pull(text)

[1] "Respected sir, Hospital staff demanded Rs 3000 from us for medicines although treatment is free under the scheme . Mobile no [PHONE] ."      
[2] "Sir ji, Hospital staff demanded Rs 1500 from us for medicines although treatment is free under the scheme. Mobile no [PHONE]."               
[3] "Respected sir, Agent of the hospital told villagers that free operation is available, took us in their van on Sunday. Contact me on [PHONE]."








WarningCaution



Audit teams must handle personal data with great care. Mask phone numbers, Aadhaar numbers, bank account numbers and names before the data leaves the secure environment. Never put such data into online tools, including AI chatbots, unless your organisation explicitly permits it.



48.4.2 Basic cleaning
Next, we make the text uniform. We convert it to lower case, remove the masked phone tags and other numbers, and squeeze extra spaces. All these functions come from stringr, which we learnt in Chapter 18.

grievances &lt;- grievances |&gt;
  mutate(clean_text = text |&gt;
           str_to_lower() |&gt;
           str_remove_all("\\[phone\\]") |&gt;
           str_remove_all("[0-9]+") |&gt;
           str_squish())

grievances |&gt;
  select(text, clean_text) |&gt;
  slice(17)

# A tibble: 1 × 2
  text                                                                clean_text
  &lt;chr&gt;                                                               &lt;chr&gt;     
1 Agent of the hospital told villagers that free opration is availab… agent of …


We have kept the original text column untouched, and created a new clean_text column. This is a good habit. We can always go back to the original to read the full complaint.
Should we remove numbers? It depends on the question. Here we only want words. But if we wanted to know how much money was demanded, the numbers would be the most important part. We could extract them first with str_extract().

49.7 Problem 1, negation
Look at these comments, and the scores they got.

feedback_scored |&gt;
  filter(str_detect(comment, "\\bnot\\b")) |&gt;
  distinct(comment, score) |&gt;
  head(6)

# A tibble: 6 × 2
  comment                                                                  score
  &lt;chr&gt;                                                                    &lt;int&gt;
1 Food was not good.  Thank you, the operation was successful.                 3
2 Food was not good.  Treatment was done.                                      1
3 Toilets were not clean.  Treatment was done.                                 1
4 Staff was rude and careless.  Doctor was not available, very poor servi…    -2
5 Discharged after treatment.  Doctor was not available, very poor servic…     0
6 Toilets were not clean.  Very clean ward and good food.                      3


“Food was not good” got a positive score. The method saw the word good, found it in the dictionary as positive, and ignored the not before it. This is the biggest weakness of simple word counting. Let us see how often it happens. We use bigrams, from the last chapter, and look for a negative word followed by a dictionary word.

negations &lt;- c("not", "no", "never", "without")

feedback |&gt;
  unnest_tokens(bigram, comment, token = "ngrams", n = 2) |&gt;
  separate_wider_delim(bigram, delim = " ", names = c("word1", "word2")) |&gt;
  filter(word1 %in% negations) |&gt;
  inner_join(bing, by = c(word2 = "word")) |&gt;
  count(word1, word2, sentiment, sort = TRUE)

# A tibble: 6 × 4
  word1 word2     sentiment     n
  &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt;     &lt;chr&gt;     &lt;int&gt;
1 not   available positive     66
2 not   happy     positive     47
3 not   free      positive     45
4 not   good      positive     39
5 not   clean     positive     38
6 not   helpful   positive     35


Hundreds of positive words were really used in a negative sense. Not available, not happy, not free, not good, not clean, not helpful.

49.7.1 Fixing negation
A simple fix is to look at the word just before each dictionary word. If it is a negation, we flip the sign. lag() gives the previous word within each comment.

feedback_words_fixed &lt;- feedback |&gt;
  unnest_tokens(word, comment) |&gt;
  mutate(previous = lag(word), .by = feedback_id) |&gt;
  inner_join(bing, by = "word") |&gt;
  mutate(value = if_else(sentiment == "positive", 1, -1),
         value = if_else(previous %in% negations, -value, value))

feedback_words_fixed |&gt;
  filter(previous %in% negations) |&gt;
  select(feedback_id, previous, word, sentiment, value) |&gt;
  head(5)

# A tibble: 5 × 5
  feedback_id previous word  sentiment value
  &lt;chr&gt;       &lt;chr&gt;    &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt;     &lt;dbl&gt;
1 FB0001      not      good  positive     -1
2 FB0008      not      happy positive     -1
3 FB0010      not      happy positive     -1
4 FB0014      not      good  positive     -1
5 FB0020      not      clean positive     -1


Note that we added the previous column before joining with the dictionary. If we joined first, the words not in the dictionary, like not itself, would already have been dropped.
Now the score of a comment is simply the sum of these values.

score_comments &lt;- function(word_values) {
  feedback |&gt;
    left_join(word_values |&gt; summarise(score = sum(value), .by = feedback_id),
              by = "feedback_id") |&gt;
    mutate(score = replace_na(score, 0))
}

feedback_fixed &lt;- score_comments(feedback_words_fixed)

cor(feedback_fixed$score, feedback_fixed$rating)

[1] 0.7814317


We wrote a small function, score_comments(), because we will use the same steps again. Custom functions were covered in Section 3.1. The correlation with the star rating has improved to 0.78.

53.7 Joining by location
In ordinary joins, rows are matched by a common value, like a hospital ID. In a spatial join, rows are matched by location. st_join() finds, for each point, the polygon it lies in, and attaches that polygon’s columns.
Our hospital table already has a district column, entered when the hospital was empanelled. A spatial join lets us check whether each hospital really lies in the district recorded against it.

hosp_check &lt;- hosp_sf |&gt;
  st_join(districts |&gt; select(district_on_map = district))

hosp_check |&gt;
  st_drop_geometry() |&gt;
  count(match = district == district_on_map)

# A tibble: 1 × 2
  match     n
  &lt;lgl&gt; &lt;int&gt;
1 TRUE    120


st_drop_geometry() turns the sf object back into an ordinary data frame, which prints more neatly. Here every hospital lies in its recorded district. In real data, such a check often finds mismatches. Some are errors in entering the district. Others may be beneficiaries or units recorded in one district to claim a benefit available only there.
